### Did federal U.S. law in 2020 require that all ballots received after Election Day be rejected and not counted?
Answer: No, federal U.S. law did not require that all ballots received after Election Day in 2020 be rejected. Election administration in the U.S. is decentralized, with each state setting its own rules. The search result notes that "a number of states only require ballots to be postmarked by Nov. 3," meaning that "many valid ballots will still be in the mail on election night in those states." Additionally, six states (California, Massachusetts, Minnesota, Mississippi, Nevada, and New York) "extended the deadline for mail ballots to be sent or received," confirming that ballots received after Election Day could still be counted in many jurisdictions.

Source URL: https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/why-were-planning-for-an-election-day-that-could-last-months/

### Did all 50 U.S. states have identical rules in 2020 regarding whether ballots postmarked on or before Election Day but received afterward would be counted?
Answer: No, the rules were not identical across all 50 states in 2020. The search results show significant variation: some states (like California, Georgia, Iowa, Maryland, Massachusetts, Minnesota, Nevada, New York, Pennsylvania, Texas, Virginia, Washington, and Wisconsin) allowed ballots postmarked by Election Day to be received and counted days or even weeks afterward (e.g., California accepted ballots received by November 20, while Maryland accepted them until November 13), while many other states (such as Alabama, Arizona, Arkansas, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Florida, Idaho, Indiana, Louisiana, Maine, Michigan, Missouri, Montana, Nebraska, New Hampshire, New Jersey, New Mexico, North Carolina, Oklahoma, Oregon, Rhode Island, South Carolina, South Dakota, Tennessee, Vermont, and Wyoming) required all ballots to be received by Election Day itself, meaning postmarked-but-late ballots would not be counted.

