### Justification
Eric Holder's claim that "all USA ballots arriving after Election Day will be thrown out" is false and constitutes cherry-picking. U.S. election administration is decentralized, with each state setting its own rules, and numerous states—including California, Illinois, Maryland, Pennsylvania, and Washington—explicitly allowed mail-in/absentee ballots postmarked on or before Election Day to be received and counted days or even weeks afterward, as documented by [FiveThirtyEight](https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/why-were-planning-for-an-election-day-that-could-last-months/), [CBS News](https://www.cbsnews.com/pictures/how-to-vote-by-mail-in-every-state-and-u-s-territory/), and the [Chicago Board of Elections](https://chicagoelections.gov/voting/vote-mail). The U.S. Supreme Court reinforced this variability by issuing a 4-4 decision allowing Pennsylvania to count ballots received up to three days after Election Day ([NACo](https://www.naco.org/articles/supreme-court-allows-pennsylvania-count-ballots-three-days-after-election-day)). While it is true that many states required ballots to be received by Election Day, Holder's sweeping, unqualified assertion omits these significant state-level exceptions, presenting a partial truth as if it applied universally to all ballots across all states.